.TH KOSH 5 "2026-10-07" "kosh" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
kosh.conf \- Koshka settings files and the KOSHCONF encoding
.SH DESCRIPTION
The settings Koshka owns live in koshconf files. They hold option values, not
shell commands, and every shell reads them before any shell startup file,
whether or not it is interactive.
.B koshconf
in
.BR kosh (1)
reads, changes, and writes them.
.PP
Shell code that should run at startup belongs in the startup files of the
.B bash
or
.B sh
mood listed under FILES. The
.B kosh
mood sources no shell startup file.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/kosh.conf
The system settings file, read first.
.TP
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kosh/kosh.conf " or " ~/.config/kosh/kosh.conf
The user settings file, read second. XDG_CONFIG_HOME is used when it is set to
an absolute path.
.B koshconf create
and
.B koshconf set \-\-persist
write this file. When the path is a symbolic link, as a dotfile manager such as
stow leaves it, the link is kept and its final target is written instead. The
new text goes to a temporary file in the target's directory, which is flushed
to disk and renamed over the target, so a reader sees either the old or the new
file. The directory is created when it is missing. The written file keeps the
permission bits of the file it replaces, and a new file is created with mode
0644 less the file creation mask. Each write holds an advisory lock on the
target's directory while it reads and replaces the file, so concurrent
.B koshconf set \-\-persist
commands that change different options all take effect. On Windows the lock is
a hidden
.I .kosh\-flock.lock
file in that directory, and only the owner write bit of the mode is kept.
.TP
.I /etc/profile
The system login file of the
.B bash
and
.B sh
moods.
.TP
.I ~/.profile
The user POSIX login file and the last Bash login candidate.
.TP
.I ~/.bash_profile
The first user Bash login candidate.
.TP
.I ~/.bash_login
The second user Bash login candidate.
.TP
.IR /etc/bash/bashrc " or " /etc/bash.bashrc
The first readable path is the system rc file of an interactive non-login Bash
mood.
.TP
.I ~/.bashrc
The default user rc file of an interactive non-login Bash mood.
.TP
.I /usr/share/bash-completion/bash_completion
Bash programmable completion, sourced after the Bash startup chain sources an rc
file. It is skipped when completion is disabled, when a default completion
specification exists, and when BASH_COMPLETION_VERSINFO is already set.
.PP
The
.B \-\-rcfile
and
.B \-\-init\-file
options and the ENV and BASH_ENV variables may select a different shell startup
file in the
.B bash
and
.B sh
moods. See
.BR kosh (1)
for their conditions and expansion rules.
.SH SYNTAX
A settings file is a sequence of lines. LF and CRLF end a line. A UTF-8 byte
order mark at the start of the file is skipped. A line that is empty, holds only
blanks, or starts with a number sign after optional blanks is ignored.
.PP
Every other line has the form
.IR name = value .
Blanks around the name are ignored. A value that starts with a double or a single
quote ends at the next quote of the same kind, and only blanks may follow it.
Otherwise the value is everything after the equals sign up to the end of the
line, blanks included. A value cannot span lines.
.PP
A boolean is written as on or off and is also read as true, false, 1, or 0. An
enumeration is written by name. A string is taken as written, and must be valid
UTF-8 without a NUL byte. history.size is a non-negative decimal integer.
.B koshconf
quotes a written value that starts with a quote or starts or ends with a blank.
A value that holds a newline, or both quote characters and a reason to quote,
cannot be written to a file, so
.B koshconf set \-\-persist
rejects it before changing the session.
.B koshconf list
shows such a value, and any value with a control byte or invalid UTF-8, in the
.B $\(aq...\(aq
form of the shell.
.PP
Only the mood and the interactive options may appear in a file. The options
that change evaluation are set for a session with
.BR set ,
.BR shopt ,
or
.BR "koshconf set" .
A later line replaces an earlier one, and the user file and KOSHCONF replace the
system file.
.SH KOSHCONF
.B KOSHCONF
is a dynamic shell variable that always holds the current settings in a binary
form, encoded as standard base64 with padding. It is never exported by itself.
After
.BR "export KOSHCONF" ,
each command the shell starts receives the settings current when it starts,
not a copy taken at the export. Another shell receives it through
.B koshconf load
.I value
or through a KOSHCONF variable in its environment, which is applied after the
settings files and removed before any command runs:
.PP
.RS
.EX
ssh host KOSHCONF="$KOSHCONF" kosh
.EE
.RE
.PP
The decoded bytes are a sequence of records. Each record is an option id, a
value length, and the value. The id and the length are unsigned LEB128 varints:
seven bits per byte, least significant group first, with the high bit set on
every byte but the last. A boolean value is one byte, 0 or 1. An enumeration is
a varint holding the value's position in its list. A string is its UTF-8 bytes.
The ids are listed by
.BR kosh (1)
and never change meaning. Only the mood and the interactive options are
written.
.PP
A reader skips a record with an unknown id, an option it never carries, or a
value that a settings file would reject, and reports a warning. Only the
canonical form is accepted: the base64 text must carry its padding, every varint
must use the fewest bytes, and the ids must strictly ascend, which is the order
the writer uses. A blob that breaks any of these rules, or whose records are
truncated, is rejected as a whole with a warning.
.SH ERRORS
A missing settings file is skipped. An unreadable one produces a warning. A line
without an equals sign, an unknown option name, an invalid value, an unclosed
quote, text after a closing quote, and an option that changes evaluation each
produce a warning that names the file and line, and the line is skipped. A
warning writes a control byte or an invalid UTF-8 byte of the file as an escape
in its message and as a question mark in its source excerpt. A settings file
never stops the shell.
.PP
Shell startup files produce the ordinary shell diagnostics described in
.BR kosh (1).
.SH MIGRATION
Earlier releases read /etc/koshrc and ~/.koshrc as shell code in the
.B kosh
mood. They are no longer read. Move option settings into
.I kosh.conf
with
.B "koshconf set"
.I option value
.BR \-\-persist ,
and move shell code into ~/.bashrc with
.B mood=bash
or with
.B \-L bash
on the command line. Guard Koshka-only lines with
.B test \-n \(dq$KOSH\(dq
and lines for other shells with
.BR "test \-z \(dq$KOSH\(dq" .
KOSH is set in every mood and never exported.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kosh (1)
